[{:seon.task/name "the-system-process-loads-only-from-commit-notices"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Runtime: the system process's one load path, load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, from empty; one collection before each fork; test fixtures made once a program; a test child takes its parent's config. Deletes: the fork server's replica role, standby, static!, static-due?, :seon.config.agent/inherited-bytes, warm-start's per-fork ctx and build.\nTHE FLOW CENSUS (owner, 2026-11-16: every process a Flow process; docs/flow.md#every-process-is-a-flow-process): runtime: (seon.agent/state is flow's: seon.step/roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph, agreed 2026-11-16) REPL runs inside the :repl proc's step, never on their own threads (seon.repl/serve-item!, drive-run); the fiber pump's io-thread (seon.agent) and the exit watcher folded into the graph; clones (seon.agent/clone!) forked by the system process, or deleted; the standby fork server and root's :log replay deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fresh-agent-holds-only-what-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (point 3 with deps). Owner's order, 2026-11-16: no patches to the freeze; work out what should happen from first principles. CAUSE [M]: a fresh agent (of2cf0671ab7fe08) is 2.48 GB live, 2.31 GB of it Chez's static generation, which is never traced or collected; lib/static! moves the fork server's live heap there before a fork when static-due? (each program move, or more than :seon.config.agent/inherited-bytes, 16 MiB, outside static), so every replaced db value, reach graph and index stays for the server's life: 1.62 GB at a morning agent, 2.31-2.39 GB the same day; only 0.55 GB of it is reachable from any var (context, result/d2d0dc39, result/d0b7feb8). Static exists because a fork shares pages copy-on-write and a copying collection in an agent would trace and so privatize every inherited object; it does that by never collecting, which is the wrong trade. PRIOR WORK: seon.fork-server/static-due?, preload, warm-start, graph-at-fork, index-at-fork (fork_server.clj); seon.lib/static! (lib.clj:177, (collect (collect-maximum-generation) 'static)); seon.db/preload! (the fork server decodes all of main at its start); seon.agent/hold-env!; docs/machine.md#what-a-process-owns-in-memory. Searched the tasks for static, inherited, preload: this task's siblings name the freeze as the design; none replaces it. LOWER BOUND (arithmetic): what a fresh agent must hold: its compiled program (~6,500 vars and the libraries, ~100-200 MB; measured 237-467 MB), its compiled tests (~2,350 deftests, ~30-50 MB; measured clojure.test/registry +206 MB, so a test holds something it should not), one db value (a root of a persistent index, ~1 KB) and the decoded nodes it reads (its task, cited sections, the forms it touches, ~10k datoms, a few MB; measured 257 MB: preload! decodes all of main), the reach graph (~6,500 rows of id, digest and ~10 refs, ~10-15 MB; measured 70 MB), the schema registry (~660 entries, 16 MB measured): ~250 MB, against 2.48 GB. A fork copies page tables, O(resident pages): ~64k pages at 250 MB, single-digit ms; ~600k at 2.5 GB. A test child: its own store connection (~5 ms measured) and its branch's config computed once by its parent (~1 ms; today hold-env! rebuilds it cold, ~175 ms), then ~21 ms a test (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound). A merge in the fork server: O(d) to apply d datoms and load the changed forms, then one full collection of ~250 MB live, ~100-200 ms once a merge, never a fork. DESIGN, each deleting what it replaces: (1) the fork server holds only that table: no whole-db preload (an agent's reads come from the store's node cache), the tests' 206 MB found and cut, the reach graph at its size. (2) No static generation: before a fork the fork server collects fully, promoting everything live to Chez's oldest generation, and its own collector frees what a merge replaced. Deletes lib/static!, static-due?, :seon.config.agent/inherited-bytes (its job, bounding what an agent traces, is (3)'s; listed to root before it goes). (3) An agent and a test child never collect that oldest generation: their collection handler stops one generation below it, so inherited pages are never traced, never privatized (Chez's collect, collect-request-handler and collect-maximum-generation; Jolt's heap-cap collection must honour it: deps). (4) A test child's config is its parent's, computed once a branch commit and handed to it; hold-env! in the child goes. ORDER: measure first, at a fresh agent, what it touches of what it inherits (each table row's live size and its first-prove reads), then (3) with deps (the mechanism (2) needs), then (2), (1), (4). PROOFS, red first: a fresh agent's live heap at most ~300 MB, and the same after a merge (red today: 2.48 GB, growing a merge); a test child's start at most the fork bound, ~65 ms (red today: 190 ms)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\". Owner: runtime. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the boot server loads main's tests at its start (seon.fork-server/main, fork_server.clj:354, program/load-all!); root's boot asks :preload once its import is written, and seon.fork-server/preload (fork_server.clj:150) does tests, graph-at-fork, index-at-fork, warm-start (fork_server.clj:77) and lib/static! as ONE step; a :fork that arrives first runs the same pieces inline (the :fork arm, fork_server.clj:205-238). Measured 2026-11-10: a root's first agent waits 4.5 s (preload! 1.2, test load 1.1, reach graph 0.8, text index 0.7); later forks 20-54 ms; under load the warm-up ran ~35 s at ~98% CPU, holding the inbox. Cause: warm-up is one step that holds the server's inbox, and a fork that comes first pays all of it whether it uses it or not. Design: the server queues each warm piece as a message to itself (decode, tests by namespace, graph, index, text index, static) and serves a frame from root before any warm step, so no request waits longer than one piece; an agent forked before a piece is warm makes that piece itself on first use (seon.agent/base-graph agent.clj:771, reconcile-index :836, its prove's test load), derived data regenerable from the db (docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on). Under the one start and one derivation, the reach graph and the test load are keyed by program digest and the graph may go entirely once recorded reads replace it; this task keeps only the scheduling. Same cause as seon.fork-server/a-commit-is-applied-while-the-fork-server-warms (merge them). Approved apart (Point, 2026-11-10): warm-start's per-agent part moved off the fork path, on condition seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run stays green with its bound unmoved. Estimate: a root's first agent ready within a fork (~70 ms) plus its own start, flat in the program's size. Proofs, red first: seon.fork-server-test/root-s-first-agent-ready-within-fork-s; seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them (stays green). FOLDED IN (context's curation, 2026-11-15, as its own plan asked): seon.fork-server/a-commit-is-applied-while-the-fork-server-warms, the same cause: Owner: runtime. Seen 2026-11-11: \"fork lag 23952 ms\" on an idle system: the boot's 10 commits queued behind the fork server's warm-up and were applied in order as it drained; the tile's p50 over a 200-commit window then stays frozen at that backlog. What exists [M]: :applied's :lag-ms is now minus the writer's :ns at its send (fork_server.clj:203-204); root keeps a 200-entry window (system.clj:836); the warm-up is one :preload step (fork_server.clj:150, :239-243) during which the server's inbox is not read. Cause: the same as seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use (warm-up holds the inbox); and the stat mixes boot backlog with steady state. Design: the warm-up queued as steps between requests (that task), so a :tx is applied before the next warm step; the tile reads lag of commits applied after the server reported warm. Merge into that task. Estimate: a commit made during warm-up applied within its own apply time (ms), not the warm-up's (s). Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/a-commit-made-while-the-fork-server-warms-is-applied-at-once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them seon.fork-server-test/a-commit-made-while-the-fork-server-warms-is-applied-at-once seon.fork-server-test/root-s-first-agent-ready-within-fork-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: (1) :stats, :static and :preload answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (peek, fork_server.clj:532 and :553); the request is sent with its :rid stripped (fork_server.clj:528), so the server cannot echo it; after a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again (fork_server.clj:479), and an answer cut by the dead server's EOF pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. (2) release! (fork_server.clj:427) now ends the boot server's whole process group with SIGKILL before its blocking c-waitpid (lib.clj:53), so a wedged boot server no longer hangs a stop; a promoted server leads no group and is left to read its EOF; then the event reader is joined with .join 5000 (fork_server.clj:450), a clock deciding. Cause: three request-id schemes for one pattern (:rid/:tag, fx's :dest, the agent's :k); this one has none. Design: the request carries :seon.step/request-id to the server and the answer echoes it; the proc answers the waiter by id (step/pend and answered); a promoted server is killed by its pid, not left to its EOF; the reader's join waits for its EOF with no timeout (the kill guarantees it). Deletes: FIFO pairing and the .join timeout. Estimate: O(1) per answer. Proofs, red first: seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id; seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server (a promoted server that reads nothing); design guard seon.main-test/an-answer-pairs-by-its-request-id (no proc pairs answers by queue order)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id seon.main-test/an-answer-pairs-by-its-request-id}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tx-step-that-threw-on-a-map-says-which"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Seen once 2026-11-05 (standing store, before an erase), not reproduced: the fork server's step threw on :tx, nth not supported on PersistentArrayMap. What exists [M]: the :tx arm (fork_server.clj:178-189) maps (nth op 1) and (nth op 2) over the message's :tx-data for seon.doc/carry-index! (doc.clj:615), assuming each op is a vector; the writer's notice is typed :seon.db/resolved-tx (a vector of [op e a v]) in resources/seon/schemas/seon.core.edn; the step's throw is answered as :step-failed and the server serves on, so that commit's application here (and the text index carry) is lost until the next. Cause [H]: some sender puts a map in :tx-data and no contract checks the message where it is made. Design: the message from the writer to the fork server is one schema (:seon.fork-server/tx, its :tx-data :seon.db/resolved-tx), checked where the writer makes it (seon.writer), so a map is refused at its producer naming it; find the producer by running that check over a day's commits. Estimate: one validation per commit at the writer (~O(datoms)). Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/commit-tx-data-holds-map-entry-refused-where (a writer commit whose tx-data holds a map is refused at the writer, naming it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/commit-tx-data-holds-map-entry-refused-where}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-inherits-the-section-leaves-it-searches"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: a measurement and a proposal to the owner, no build. Owner: runtime. Measured 2026-11-08 (seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited): an agent's first grep allocates 8-15 MB more than its second, all cold decoding of the EAVT leaves holding sections' :seon.doc/text, which every forked agent decodes for itself; the text index itself is inherited current. Candidate: read those leaves in the fork server's warm steps (seon.fork-server/warm-start, fork_server.clj:77), so agents inherit them decoded (copy-on-write). Measure first: the fork server's resident memory with and without (docs/machine.md's budget) against bytes saved per agent times agents per hour; propose with the numbers. Folds into the warm-step queue of seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use if accepted. Estimate: saves 8-15 MB x agents forked; costs the leaves' decoded size once in the server. Proof once accepted: seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-allocates-what-its-second-does. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.doc/what-a-first-search-allocates-after-a-long-history (Found 2026-11-05 by the control's merge gates)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-allocates-what-its-second-does}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-step-that-throws-answers-every-waiting-ask"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Found 2026-11-09: a :fork step threw, root recorded :seon.fork-server/step-failed, :seon.agent/failed and :seon.agent/died, and the in-agent attach waiting on the agent the fork owed was never answered (a shard 15+ min at 0% CPU). What exists [M]: the server's step catches any throw and sends {:ev :step-failed :op :agent} (fork_server.clj:161-168, :298-301); root's api records it as a :platform problem only (system.clj:839); the spawn is ended only by :fork-failed (spawn-failed, system.clj:833-835), which a :step-failed on a :fork never triggers; answer-taken does end a lost :fork's spawn (system.clj:992-993) but only for a throw in root's own step. Cause: a fork step's failure is two events with two meanings, and only one ends the spawn. Design: a :step-failed whose :op is :fork is the fork's refusal: root answers the spawn (spawn-failed with the error), which answers every ask waiting on that agent, as seon.repl/defect-answer does for render (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs); one event, :fork-failed carrying the error, and :step-failed is for steps that owe no one. Estimate: none added. Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/fork-step-throws-answers-attach-waiting-agent-error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/fork-step-throws-answers-attach-waiting-agent-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fork-server-is-a-proc-in-a-flow-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime; flow reviews (flow's decision via deps, 2026-11-15, root's duplicate check). PRIOR WORK: seon.step/run-loop (step.clj:66) is a second step loop beside Flow's: it runs a step-fn on one thread, takes :pause/:resume/:ping/:stop as transport messages and answers pings itself; its one caller is the fork server (fork_server.clj:371, server-step). Agents' procs already run in a Flow graph of their own process (seon.repl/serve!). Jolt 39bc9c26 measures every Flow step (jolt.host/measured [pid in-port], shown in the dump), so a step outside Flow is the one step no dump names. Design: the fork server's step-fn runs as a proc in a Flow graph in its process, as seon.repl/serve! does for an agent; its socket and exit events become its in-ports; run-loop and its control-op handling are deleted. Cost: Flow's per-message dispatch (~5k ticks a step, as every proc's) against run-loop's loop; no new wait. Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/the-fork-servers-step-is-a-flow-proc (its pid answers flow/ping, and its running step is named in a fiber dump). Deletes: seon.step/run-loop and control-op."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/the-fork-servers-step-is-a-flow-proc}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.fork-server/server-step {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 5000, :bytes 4096}}}
]
